// =============================================================================
// ui/tsconfig.node.json
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Purpose:
//   TypeScript project for the Node-executed half of the ui tree: exactly two
//   files, vite.config.ts and vitest.config.ts. Vite and Vitest load those two
//   at start-up, in Node, before any browser bundle exists -- so they are the
//   only TypeScript in this tree that may legitimately reach for a Node API.
//   This project type-checks them (it never emits) and grants them the Node
//   type definitions that ui/tsconfig.json withholds from the browser sources
//   under ui/src.
//
//   Two callers consume it. ui/package.json's `typecheck` script runs
//   `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.node.json` as the second of its two invocations,
//   after the same command against the sibling app project, so both projects are
//   compiler-checked and neither config file can carry a type error that nothing
//   reports; that manifest's `build` script delegates to `npm run typecheck`, so
//   the bundle is never produced unless this project passes too, and
//   .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml invokes that same script. ui/eslint.config.js
//   is the second caller: it lists this project alongside ui/tsconfig.json in its
//   parser options so both config files are linted WITH type information rather
//   than falling out of the lint gate entirely.
//
// WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
//   - Alternatives Considered: one combined tsconfig spanning ui/src and these
//     two files was evaluated and rejected. A single project has a single
//     `types` list, so it would have to either add "node" -- putting `process`,
//     `__dirname`, `Buffer` and `require` in scope for all 21 React screens,
//     where `process.env.API_URL` inside a component would type-check cleanly
//     and then throw at run time in a browser that has no `process` -- or omit
//     "node" and leave these two files unable to type-check at all. Two
//     sibling projects are the only arrangement that types both halves without
//     lending Node globals to browser code.
//   - Alternatives Considered: the stock-Vite layout, in which the root
//     tsconfig carries only `references` and this project sets
//     `composite: true`, was rejected because here it is silently inert.
//     `tsc -p` does not traverse project references; only `tsc -b` does, and
//     nothing in this tree invokes `-b`. Every consumer of the browser project
//     reaches it through the literal `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`:
//     ui/package.json's `typecheck` script runs it directly, its `build` script
//     reaches it by delegating to `npm run typecheck`, and the type-check gate in
//     .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml -- the "Type-check application and tool
//     configuration" step, which runs `npm run typecheck` -- reaches the same
//     command a third time. Refactoring Rationale: that gate was described here as
//     "planned" while the paragraph above this list already stated, correctly, that
//     the workflow invokes the script. One file cannot hold both readings: a
//     contributor who believed the gate were still planned would have no reason to
//     keep this project reachable by a literal `-p` invocation, which is the whole
//     conclusion this entry reaches. The workflow now exists and both statements
//     are written in the present tense. Under a
//     references-only root that owns no files of its own, every one of those
//     paths would check ZERO files and still exit 0 -- a gate that reports
//     success having verified nothing. The two projects are therefore siblings,
//     each reached by its own invocation, and neither references the other.
//   - Assumptions: the compiler is typescript 6.0.3 as pinned in
//     ui/package.json, and it stays there. typescript-eslint 8.65.0
//     peer-declares `typescript >=4.8.4 <6.1.0`; raising the compiler past that
//     ceiling stops the type-aware lint pass -- and with it the JSDoc
//     documentation gate -- while the build stays green, so the loss is
//     invisible to whoever raised it.
// =============================================================================
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Assumptions: ES2022 matches the Node engine range pinned by package.json;
    // these files never ship to browsers, so down-level output buys nothing.
    "target": "ES2022",

    // Trade-offs: omitting DOM libraries prevents browser-only globals from
    // type-checking in files that execute under Node.
    "lib": ["ES2022"],

    // Assumptions: package.json selects ESM and Vite/Vitest resolve imports
    // through their bundler, so the checker mirrors that loader.
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // Assumptions: only these two Node-executed files receive @types/node;
    // ui/tsconfig.json intentionally withholds those globals from browser code.
    "types": ["node"],

    // Assumptions: Vite and Vitest transpile their own configuration; tsc only
    // checks types and must not create an unused JavaScript tree.
    "noEmit": true,

    // Trade-offs: strict checking adds little cost across two files and catches
    // malformed Vite or Vitest options at their declaration.
    "strict": true,

    // Trade-offs: these checks catch half-finished configuration edits that
    // `strict` alone permits.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,

    // Assumptions: esbuild transforms files independently, so isolated modules
    // and verbatim syntax keep type-only imports explicit.
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,

    // Trade-offs: dependency declarations are trusted so the check stays
    // focused on the two local configuration files.
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // Assumptions: CommonJS packages in the toolchain require default-import
    // interoperability consistent with the runtime loader.
    "esModuleInterop": true,

    // Assumptions: case-sensitive build environments must reject import casing
    // that a developer filesystem might tolerate.
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    // Assumptions: configuration may import declarative JSON such as package
    // metadata and should receive its actual inferred shape.
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    // Assumptions: both members are modules even if a future edit removes their
    // last explicit import or export.
    "moduleDetection": "force"
  },

  // WHY : Assumptions: the three files are named literally rather than matched by
  // a glob such as `*.ts`. This project is the one place in the ui tree that
  // hands out Node types, so its membership has to stay a deliberate list:
  // under a glob the next root-level .ts file anyone adds would join it
  // silently and inherit `process` and `Buffer` without that ever being
  // chosen. Widening it should require editing this line. No `exclude`
  // accompanies it -- a three-entry list is already closed, and a second bound
  // would only raise the question of which one wins.
  // WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the list carried two entries and now carries
  // three. `documentationGate.test.ts` is the negative-probe suite for the JSDoc
  // gate in `eslint.config.js`; it writes deliberately non-conforming sources to
  // disk, lints them through the ESLint Node API and removes them, so it needs
  // `node:fs`, `node:path` and `node:url`. It belongs HERE and not under `src`
  // because `tsconfig.json` sets `"types": []` on purpose -- ambient types are
  // declared per project, so admitting Node's declarations to the project that
  // covers `src` would hand `process` and `Buffer` to all 21 screens. A
  // file-local `/// <reference types="node" />` was considered and rejected for
  // the same reason: a triple-slash reference joins the whole PROGRAM, so the
  // screens would acquire the globals regardless and the narrowing would be
  // appearance only.
  // WHY : Trade-offs: all three named members exist and `tsc -p` checks all
  // three, so the accepted cost of a literal list is only that a fourth Node
  // configuration does not join automatically; adding one requires editing this
  // line. That is also the benefit: a glob cannot silently grant Node globals to
  // a new root-level TypeScript file. The rejected references-only layout would
  // have avoided the editing cost only by being silently inert, which is
  // precisely the failure mode this project is shaped to prevent: a project that
  // checks nothing still exits 0.
  // WHY : Trade-offs: a TEST is now a member of the build-tooling project, which
  // reads oddly beside the screen tests under `src`. It is the honest grouping --
  // what that suite exercises is the lint configuration rather than the
  // application -- and the alternative was the ambient-type leak above.
  "include": ["vite.config.ts", "vitest.config.ts", "documentationGate.test.ts"]
}
